---
/*
 * The homepage's door into the settings guide — Ukrainian-only, so it renders
 * on `/uk` and nowhere else. Rationale for its existence and for the shape of
 * its copy lives with the strings, in `lib/guide.ts` under `guideStrings.teaser`.
 *
 * ## Why this is a section and not a panel
 *
 * The first build of this was a tinted card with a rounded border and a solid
 * green button, and it carried a red chip counting the faults in the reader's
 * language list. Read back, that is two bad shapes at once. A claim about your
 * device plus a red problem count plus one button to fix it is the grammar of
 * scareware, which is a poor thing to build on a site whose entire proposition
 * is that nothing leaves your browser. And a tinted card with a CTA button,
 * dropped between two content sections, is the shape of an ad slot — which
 * readers skip on reflex, so it would have failed even where it was honest.
 *
 * So it is built like the sections around it: the same full-bleed shell as
 * `Stakes`, the same eyebrow and heading scale, and the same closing text link
 * those sections use instead of a button.
 *
 * ## Why it shows the list instead of counting its faults
 *
 * A verdict is an assertion the reader has to take on faith, which is exactly
 * what a scam offers. The list is a fact they can check against their own
 * settings in ten seconds, and it makes the same point without claiming
 * anything: someone whose list still carries Russian can see that it does.
 *
 * `GuideChecker` is the surface that grades the list, and it earns that — a
 * reader who opened «Як зробити українську мовою за замовчуванням» has asked
 * the question. This has not been asked, so it answers a smaller one. Note the
 * hub's own count strip is untoned mono for the same reason; the red was
 * invented here and had no precedent.
 *
 * ## Why the line under the list is this section's own
 *
 * Everything else here defers to `guide-diagnosis`, and the reckoning still
 * does. The words do not: the hub's privacy line says «на вашому пристрої»,
 * which is a different claim from «прочитано тут, поки ви читаєте» — and
 * without the second one, three chips read as an illustration rather than as
 * your list, read a second ago. `guideStrings.teaser` carries the reasoning,
 * including the one thing these words must never say.
 */
import { GUIDE_INDEX_HREF, guideStrings } from '../lib/guide';

const t = guideStrings.teaser;
---

<section id="language-list" class="border-t border-border bg-bg px-6 py-20">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl">
    <div class="max-w-3xl">
      <p class="font-display text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-label text-accent-text">
        {t.eyebrow}
      </p>
      <h2
        class="mt-3 font-display text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-display text-ink-strong sm:text-4xl"
      >
        {t.heading}
      </h2>
    </div>

    {/*
     * Empty and `hidden` until the script fills it. Without JavaScript — and in
     * the private windows and Safari builds that hide the list from the page on
     * purpose — the section is its heading and its link, which still names the
     * guide and still says what the guide is for.
     *
     * No rule above the list and no label on it: the heading directly above
     * names both the languages and their order, and these rows are what both
     * halves of it point at, so the two are one sentence and a label between
     * them would interrupt it.
     *
     * ## Why it is inline, and an ordered list
     *
     * It was a row of chips first, which is the wrong family — chips are tags
     * and filters: a set, unordered, each one equal and usually removable,
     * where this is ranked and the rank is the entire point. Then it was a
     * bordered card of stacked rows, which fixed the rank and broke two other
     * things. A card interrupts the sentence: these rows ARE the «цими
     * мовами» the heading names, so a boxed widget between the two halves
     * stops the reader mid-clause. And a bordered list of rows with
     * dividers is what an editable settings control looks like, while nothing
     * here can be reordered or removed — an affordance the section cannot
     * honour.
     *
     * The ranking is stated in the heading rather than by rank glyphs on the
     * row — see `guideStrings.teaser.heading`, which absorbed the separate
     * «У порядку пріоритету» label that used to sit here. That label is why
     * the list has no rule line of its own any more: the sentence the reader
     * has just read already carries it, and re-stating it directly above the
     * rows put a caption between a heading and the phrase completing it.
     * Everything below is about the row itself.
     *
     * So: inline, no box, reading on from the heading. The arrow is the rank,
     * and it is the mechanism rather than decoration — a site walks the list
     * and serves the first language it has, which is what «українська →
     * російська» describes and why a reader with Russian second should find it
     * uncomfortable. It is `aria-hidden`; the `<ol>` carries the order for
     * anyone not seeing it.
     *
     * The first item takes the strong ink because it is factually the one
     * sites take. That is position, not praise, so it stays true for a reader
     * whose first is not Ukrainian, and nothing marks any item as bad — see
     * `guideStrings.teaser` on what this section is not allowed to become.
     */}
    <div data-guide-list hidden class="mt-6">
      <ol
        data-guide-languages
        class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 gap-y-1 text-lg"
      ></ol>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-ink-medium">{t.listSource}</p>
    </div>

    <a
      href={GUIDE_INDEX_HREF}
      class="mt-10 inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm font-semibold text-accent-text hover:underline"
    >
      {guideStrings.index.title}
      <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    </a>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  import { guideLanguages } from '../lib/guide-diagnosis';

  const block = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-guide-list]');
  const list = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-guide-languages]');

  if (block && list) {
    const tags = navigator.languages?.length
      ? [...navigator.languages]
      : navigator.language
        ? [navigator.language]
        : [];

    const languages = guideLanguages(tags);

    /*
     * Nothing to show is not the same as nothing wrong, and this section has no
     * room to explain the difference — so where the browser says nothing, the
     * block simply stays hidden and the guide's own checker handles that case.
     */
    if (languages.length > 0) {
      for (const language of languages) {
        const item = document.createElement('li');
        item.className = 'flex items-baseline gap-3';

        /* The first item carries the weight because it is the one sites take —
           a statement about position, which stays true whichever language is
           in it. No role colouring anywhere: marking Russian would put back the
           alarm this section was rebuilt to remove, and the reader can see
           perfectly well what is in their own list. */
        /* The arrow LEADS its item rather than trailing the one before it, and
           the difference only shows when the row wraps: with trailing arrows a
           wrapped line ends on an arrow pointing at empty space, where a
           leading one opens the next line as the continuation it is. Found at
           390px with eight languages, which is an ordinary list — Chrome adds a
           regional variant per keyboard layout. */
        if (language.rank > 1) {
          const arrow = document.createElement('span');
          arrow.className = 'text-ink-faint';
          arrow.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
          arrow.textContent = '→';
          item.append(arrow);
        }

        const name = document.createElement('span');
        name.className =
          language.rank === 1 ? 'font-semibold text-ink-strong' : 'text-ink-soft';
        name.textContent = language.name;
        item.append(name);

        list.append(item);
      }
      block.hidden = false;
    }
  }
</script>
